<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Computing work</title>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Imagine that a cable, 50 meters long and having
  mass 150 kilograms, is hanging from the top of a building. We will
  compute the amount of work needed to lift the entire cable to the
  top of the building.</p>
        <p>Assuming that the cable is uniform, how much mass is in every meter
  of the cable?  That is, what is the density of the cable in
  kilograms per meter?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Let's set up a coordinate system: <m>y</m> will measure the
  vertical distance upwards from the bottom of the cable.</p>
        <p><image source="figures/cable15.eps"/></p>
        <p>Imagine that we take a super thin slice of width <m>dy</m> at location
  <m>y</m>.  Sketch the slice above and use the density to determine the mass
  <m>dm</m> of this slice.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Imagine that the segment is very thin.  How far does this segment
  need to be lifted when <m>y = 10</m>?  <m>y = 20</m>?  <m>y = 30</m>?  for a
  general value of <m>y</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How much work <m>dW</m> is performed in lifting just this segment to the top
  of the building?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now that you know the amount of work required to lift each segment
  to the top of the building, determine the total work required to
  raise the entire cable to the top of the building.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>Here is a similar problem.  Consider a cone whose height and
  base radius are each 2 meters.  You may imagine creating this cone
  by taking 
  the line <m>y=x</m> between <m>0\leq x\leq 2</m> and spinning it about the
  <m>y</m>-axis.  Now imagine that this cone is filled
  with water and we want to know how much work is required to pump the
  water up to the top of the cone.</p>
        <p>Imagine we take a thin horizontal cylindrical slice of the cone at position
  <m>y</m> and of height <m>dy</m>.  What is the volume <m>dV</m> of this slice?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>The density of water is <m>1000</m> kg/m<m>^3</m>.  What is
  the mass <m>dm</m> of this slice?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>How far does this slice need to be lifted to raise it to the top of
  the cone?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.7in">
      <statement>
        <p>How much work <m>dW</m> is required to raise this slice to the top of the
  cone?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How much work is required to pump all the water out of the cone?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose now that we have a spherical water tank of radius 3
  meters.  You may imagine forming this tank by spinning the right
  half of a circle <m>x^2+y^2=3^2=9</m> about the <m>y</m>-axis.  Suppose that
  the tank is filled with water to a depth of one meter and that the
  density of the water is <m>1000-y</m> kilograms per cubic meter.</p>
        <p><image source="figures/21-sphere.eps"/></p>
        <p>Find the volume of water in the tank.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the center of mass of water in the tank.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the amount of work required to pump all of the water to the top
  of the tank.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
